p=O/'physics-development.html';s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig');add='''<h2>Effective bank-response candidate</h2><figure><img src="iteration-effective-bank.png" alt="Eight withheld bank histories compared with the effective bank-response model"><figcaption>One parameter set across both masses. Training uses the same thirteen ordinary manual-turn windows as the two-body fit. The six previously examined development maneuvers plus two additional ordinary cases are shown. Only bank is predicted here: this is not a validated full simulator. Initial airspeed is held constant within each three-second forecast.</figcaption></figure><pre>φ̈ = K (V₀/5)² (1.385/m) δf − k sin φ − d φ̇ + b u
τ δ̇f = δcmd − δf
δcmd = max(right_pull − sR, 0) − max(left_pull − sL, 0)</pre><p>The aerodynamic input scales with dynamic pressure divided by mass; restoring torque is bounded by sin(bank), and damping opposes roll rate. Parameters are effective identified coefficients, not direct measurements of inertia or aerodynamics. Fitted bank RMS error is 4.2 degrees across training windows and 2.5–9.0 degrees across the eight displayed validation windows. The fitted response time (0.343 s) combines actuator and canopy response and must not be described as a measured servo time constant. The left threshold reaches about 20 mm, near its search bound, versus about 0.9 mm right. This is a hypothesis about effective brake response, not a confirmed physical slack measurement; a threshold-free ablation and identifiability check are required.</p><p>Source and results: <code>fit_effective_bank.py</code>, <code>effective-bank-fit.json</code>. This promising bank law must next be coupled to force-balanced airspeed, climb and turn dynamics; it cannot yet justify a radius envelope or controller gains.</p>''';s=s.replace('</html>',add+'</html>');p.write_text(s)
